      "text": "To solve the given system of equations \\( x + y = 4 \\) and \\( 3xy = 4 \\), we start by expressing \\( y \\) in terms of \\( x \\) from the first equation:\n\n\\[ y = 4 - x. \\]\n\nNext, substitute \\( y = 4 - x \\) into the second equation:\n\n\\[ 3x(4 - x) = 4. \\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[ 12x - 3x^2 = 4, \\]\n\\[ 3x^2 - 12x + 4 = 0. \\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 3 \\), \\( b = -12 \\), and \\( c = 4 \\):\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{(-12)^2 - 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4}}{2 \\cdot 3} = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{144 - 48}}{6} = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{96}}{6} = \\frac{12 \\pm 4\\sqrt{6}}{6} = \\frac{6 \\pm 2\\sqrt{6}}{3} = 2 \\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}. \\]\n\nThus, the solutions for \\( x \\) are:\n\n\\[ x = 2 + \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = 2 - \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}. \\]\n\nWe recognize that \\( 2 + \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3} = \\frac{6 + 2\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\) and \\( 2 - \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3} = \\frac{6 - 2\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\). Therefore, the solutions are:\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{6 + 2\\sqrt{6}}{3}, \\quad y = 2 - \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}, \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{6 - 2\\sqrt{6}}{3}, \\quad y = 2 + \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}. \\]\n\nIn the form \\( x = \\frac{a \\pm b\\sqrt{c}}{d} \\), we identify \\( a = 6 \\), \\( b = 2 \\), \\( c = 6 \\), and \\( d = 3 \\). Thus, the value of \\( a + b + c + d \\) is:\n\n\\[ 6 + 2 + 6 + 3 = 17. \\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{17}. \\]",
